<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theodoros Kolokotronis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1770">1770</a>–<a href="/years/1843">1843</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Theodoros Kolokotronis was the leading military commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-war-of-independence">Greek War of Independence</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>. A former klepht who had served with British forces in the Ionian Islands, he directed the siege and capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli-greece">Tripolitsa</a> in 1821 and destroyed the Ottoman army of Dramali Pasha at the Dervenakia pass in 1822. He clashed with the civilian leaders, was imprisoned during the civil strife of 1824–25 and was sentenced to death under King Otto's regency before being pardoned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theodoros-kolokotronis.jpg" alt="Theodoros Kolokotronis" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dionysios Tsokos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kolokotronis01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Theodoros Kolokotronis was born on 3 April 1770 at Ramavouni, a mountain in Messenia, into a family of klephts. His father, Konstantinos, a former captain of the Armatoloi in Corinth, took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orlov-revolt">Orlov Revolt</a>, which was encouraged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and Theodoros was named in honour of Fyodor Orlov. His father was killed in 1780 in a fight with Ottoman troops. At 15 he was taken in by Mitros Petrovas, who taught him his first lessons in fighting. Before the revolution he worked as a klepht, an armatolos and a kápos for Peloponnesian notables.</p><p>Ottoman attacks on the klephts drove him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakynthos">Zakynthos</a> in 1806. There, under British occupation, he served under the philhellene Richard Church in the 1st Regiment Greek Light Infantry, became a major in 1810 and took the red helmet he later wore. In the Heptanese he met the revolutionary ideas of the age, which he later credited with opening the eyes of the world.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He returned to the mainland just before the war began in March 1821 and formed a confederation of klepht bands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>. In May 1821 he was named commander-in-chief, and his age of 50 helped win him the name O Geros tou Morea, the Elder of Morea. He led the Greek forces in the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli-greece">Tripolitsa</a>. In 1822 an Ottoman army under Mahmud Dramali Pasha took Corinth and entered the plain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a>, but the Greek fleet under Andreas Miaoulis cut it off from its supply ships. When Dramali retreated through the Dervenaki Pass, Kolokotronis's quicker forces trapped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> there in August 1822 and annihilated them in the Battle of Dervenakia.</p><p>He then took part in the civil wars among the Greek factions, and when his party was defeated he was jailed on Hydra in March 1825. His eldest son, Panos, was killed in the second civil war. He was released when an Egyptian army under Ibrahim Pasha invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, and in 1825 he was appointed commander-in-chief of the Greek forces there. Using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> tactics and scorched earth, he could not prevent the destruction Ibrahim left behind.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the war Kolokotronis supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannis-kapodistrias">Ioannis Kapodistrias</a>, and after Kapodistrias was assassinated on 8 October 1831 he backed Prince Otto of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> for the throne. He later opposed the Bavarian regency and, accused with Dimitrios Plapoutas of conspiracy, was sentenced to death for treason in 1834, then pardoned in 1835. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in 1843, a day after his son's wedding. In his last years he learned to write in order to complete memoirs, a leading account of the war. In 1930 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleftherios-venizelos">Eleftherios Venizelos</a> had his bones moved to Tripoli, and his helmet and arms are in the National Historical Museum in Athens.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Theodoros Kolokotronis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It organised supplies and taxes for the siege of Tripolitsa and quarrelled with Demetrios Ypsilantis, who claimed national leadership, and with military chiefs such as <b>Theodoros Kolokotronis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/peloponnesian-senate">Peloponnesian Senate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.98,23.73&amp;year=1817" aria-label="Athens on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Theodoros Kolokotronis"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4578 4308h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4578 4308h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=37.98,23.73&amp;year=1817">What was here in 1817? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
